        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Shaklee Life Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shaklee Life Shake Soy contains 110 calories, 20 g of protein, 1.5 g of fat, 9 g of carbohydrate, 6 g of fiber, 0 g of sugar and 510 mg of sodium per 38 g serving of French Vanilla, per the nutrition facts on Shaklee's site at the time of writing. The plant version in Rich Chocolate has 150 calories, 3 g of fat, 11 g of carbohydrate, 6 g of fiber and 400 mg of sodium in a 44 g serving. Figures vary a little by flavor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How is Life Shake sweetened?</h3>

          <p>
            Life Shake is sweetened with rebaudioside M (a stevia-leaf compound) and monk fruit extract, with natural flavors. The reformulated shake dropped added sugar entirely, and Shaklee's own FAQ notes two exceptions: the soy Strawberry and Cafe Latte flavors carry 1 g of sugar. There is no sucralose or acesulfame potassium, so it belongs alongside the picks in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does the vitamin premix add?</h3>

          <p>
            The vitamin and mineral list is long. Per the label, a serving of the soy version gives 100% of the daily value for vitamin D and vitamin B12, 50% for calcium, 35% for most B vitamins and vitamin E, 30% for vitamin K and 60% for molybdenum. Folate is listed as L-5-methyltetrahydrofolate and B12 as methylcobalamin, the methylated forms Shaklee highlights. If you already take a multivitamin, two Life Shakes a day plus your pill can push several nutrients well past the daily value, which is worth a glance at both labels.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you watch on the label?</h3>

          <p>
            Sodium is the number most reviews skip. At 510 mg per serving, the soy vanilla delivers 22% of the daily value before you add milk, which is high for a protein shake. Someone replacing two meals a day with it, as Shaklee's weight programme suggests, takes in over 1,000 mg of sodium from the shakes alone. That is fine for most people, but worth knowing if you have high blood pressure or kidney disease and have been told to limit salt. The label shows no gums, carrageenan or artificial colors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Shaklee Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shaklee Life Shake is not certified by an independent sport-testing program that we could find: it is not listed by NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or BSCG at the time of writing. Shaklee's own claim is that it runs 2,103 tests on each Life Shake production run, covering purity and potency, and backs the shake with a 100% money-back guarantee. Those are in-house quality checks reported by the brand, not a certification you can look up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters for two groups. Athletes in drug-tested sport need a product whose batches are screened by an outside lab for banned substances, and in-house testing does not meet that bar. Shoppers worried about heavy metals in plant protein, a real issue that our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide covers, have no published lead or cadmium figures from Shaklee to compare against other brands. The brand may well test for both; it simply does not publish the results.
          </p>

          <p>
            One Shaklee protein does carry outside screening. Batches of the Performance Advanced Physique grass-fed whey appear in the Banned Substances Control Group (BSCG) Drug Free database, alongside Shaklee's energy chews and pre-workout. That is a real, checkable listing. But it covers the whey, not Life Shake or Energizing Soy, and we found no BSCG, NSF or Informed Sport entry for any Shaklee soy or pea product.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Shaklee does publish is clinical research on the shake itself. The two Westcott studies it cites tracked people who replaced two meals a day with Life Shake, followed a twice-weekly exercise plan and calorie targets, and reported an average loss of 14 lb of fat with a gain of 3.7 lb of lean muscle over six months, per Shaklee. Because the shake was tested inside a whole programme, the results cannot be credited to the powder alone. For certified options, see our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> list.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TESTING VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">In-house testing, not certification</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Shaklee says it runs 2,103 tests per Life Shake batch, but no independent sport certifier lists Life Shake. Only Shaklee's whey has outside BSCG Drug Free listings. If you compete in drug-tested sport, choose a product with a certification you can look up.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

